## Reflection Questions:

**1. How did you handle state updates when the text changed?**
I used useState to store the text in the parent component. When the user types, the child component sends the new text to the parent through a callback function. The parent updates the state, and React recalculates the statistics.

**2. What considerations did you make when calculating reading time?**
I estimated the reading time assuming that an average person reads about 200 words per minute. I converted the word count into seconds and used Math.ceil to round up to the next whole second. I chose this approach to provide a simple and easy-to-understand estimate that updates as the user types. I also formatted the result as minutes and seconds to make the reading time easier to understand.

**3. How did you ensure the UI remained responsive during rapid text input?**
I used React state and the onChange event to update the statistics as the user types. I also used Tailwind CSS responsive classes to adjust the layout for different screen sizes.

4. What challenges did you face when implementing the statistics calculations?
One challenge was counting words correctly when the text was empty or contained extra spaces. I used trim() to remove leading and trailing spaces and a ternary operator (? :) to check if the text was empty before counting the words. Then, I used split(/\s+/).length to split the text using one or more whitespace characters and count the words correctly.